note.note:
coxcomb primarily refers to a rooster’s crest, or a cock’s comb. Given that jester hats resemble roosters’ crests, and jesters are fools, the word coxcomb evolved to describe not only the jester’s cap but fools themselves. The word coxcomb can also describe a vain, pretentious, and foppish person, perhaps because roosters are viewed as prideful. In other words, someone described as a coxcomb pretends to be stylish and educated. As jesters are the noble’s personal comedians and storytellers, Franville’s use takes on additional meaning. The insult could imply that Aminta’s relative is pseudo-nobility who amuses other nobles with attempts at legitimacy. Given that the men believe it is okay for superiors eat their inferiors, Franville’s disqualification of Aminta’s family may also justify his turn to cannibalism (
   OED
).

The use of sharp-set in this passage is layered. As an adjective, it describes a strong hunger (
                     OED
                  ). However, it is also a word used to describe hawks, hounds, and other hunting animals and their eagerness for predatory behavior. That said, this use of sharp-set figures the shipwrecked nobles as savage beasts. Yet another definition of sharp-set is having a craving for sexual indulgence, which also promotes the connection between food and sexual craving that appears throughout the passage. For example, with the first use of sharp-set, the men argue over who gets to eat her buttocks, and with the second use, Tibalt asks if the men are so hungry that Aminta’s flesh must serve them, here again evoking sexual indulgence. As Aminta is the only woman on the barren island, it is worth noting that the cannibalism directed towards her—i.e. to consume her—is meant to be read as a sexual metaphor.

In this passage, Albert pledges the men’s subservience to Rosella and appears to reverse the patriarchal assumptions of early modern social relations. Creatures, in this sense, likely refers to someone who owes his fortune or position, and remains subservient to, a patron, and who also is ready to do another’s bidding (
                     OED
                  ). According to Albert, then, the Frenchmen are not only willing to subjugate themselves to these women, but also are willing to do their bidding (sexually or otherwise). What makes this passage especially interesting is how quickly its effects are reversed; just ten lines later, Rosella cedes power back to the men. These quick-shifting dynamics are likely related to the comedic genre of the play, as the playwrights keep switching back and forth between giving their French protagonists patriarchal power and taking it away from them.
